      "source_example_id": "e-2182",
      "knowledge_point": "Constructing a Rotation-Scaling Matrix That Represents a Geometric Transformation",
      "kp_id": "e-2182"
    },
    "q-39391": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39391",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14243854/assets/a99414a9657a869f57ceb2cb90813b4f5b94e00ba9a9b44a5deb24587157fcfd.png)\n\nThe vectors $v$ and $Av$ are shown above. If $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, then $A=$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, then we can write\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the scale factor and $\\theta $ is the angle of rotation. \n\nAccording to the diagram, $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
